<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orkhon inscriptions</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/732">732</a>–735 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Orkhon script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orkhon inscriptions are monumental stone texts erected in the Orkhon valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> between 732 and 735 CE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a>. Written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> runic script, they commemorate rulers and statesmen and reflect on the fate of the Turkic people. They are the earliest substantial written records in any Turkic language and a key legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions.jpg" alt="Orkhon inscriptions" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vezirtonyukuk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kultigin_Monument_of_Orkhon_Inscriptions.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Orkhon Inscriptions</b>, Ilterish Qaghan began rebuilding the fallen Turkic empire in 682 with just seventeen men in the Yin Shan mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Orkhon inscriptions</b>, in the Old Turkic script of the Göktürks and other khanates of the 8th to 10th centuries, show Tengri choosing and guiding the khagan, whose deeds were done because “Heaven so ordained”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Orkhon inscriptions</b> are the first works of Turkic literature: eloquent texts, rich in parallelism and formulas of oral poetry, which combine history, political exhortation and lament.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>It split around 600 and fell to the Tang; the Second Turkic Khaganate (682–744) left the <b>Orkhon inscriptions</b>, in which Bilge Qaghan warned his people against the sweet words and soft silks of China.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
